1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,640
In this tutorial we'll cover how to use burp proxy interception rules and all of the associated

2
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,640
options.

3
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:19,780
Burp proxy is the tool that you can use to intercept, view and modify all the traffic

4
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:23,000
passing between your browser and the application you're testing.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:33,120
To see how that works I'm just going to open Burp's embedded browser and visit a URL.

6
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:38,880
When I do that the requests come through in the proxy intercept view in Burp so that you

7
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:42,120
can view, modify and forward them.

8
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:47,380
If you're testing a modern application this can result in a large number of requests and

9
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:51,760
responses and it can become a bit too much to intercept and view them all and keep having

10
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,760
to click forward.

11
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,760
You can use Burp's interception rules to control exactly what gets intercepted.

12
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:04,900
We do that in the proxy tool, in the options tab, these settings for intercept client requests

13
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,440
and intercept server responses.

14
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:11,000
To intercept anything you need to check the box in each section.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,400
By default requests are intercepted, by default responses are not intercepted and you also

16
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,920
need to have your master interception toggle on otherwise everything will get passed through

17
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,600
automatically.

18
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:29,880
You can define multiple rules to determine what gets intercepted.

19
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:33,400
Each of these rules can be individually toggled to enable or disable it.

20
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,860
You can have rules set up that you might use from time to time.

21
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,940
You don't have to have them enabled all the time.

22
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:47,140
The enabled rules are processed in order from top to bottom and the results are combined using

23
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,140
Boolean operators.

24
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:51,860
And this is a simple left to right logic with no configurable scope.

25
00:01:51,860 --> 00:02:01,140
By default for requests, Burp will intercept requests whose file extension does not match a list of

26
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:06,740
fairly boring common file extensions for images, style sheets, scripts and other things.

27
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:10,820
In general, you're less interested in those page resources when you're working.

28
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:15,380
There are some other rules that are there by default but are not enabled.

29
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:23,540
So you can say that you will intercept any requests containing parameters or if the HTTP method does

30
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:30,820
not match the usual get or post, you can enable interception only of items that are in your target

31
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:31,140
scope.

32
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:38,100
For responses, the default rule if you enable response interception is that only responses where

33
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,180
the content type header contains the word text will be intercepted.

34
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:45,060
And there are some other rules which you can use for common purposes.

35
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:51,460
So if you want to always intercept a response when you modified or intercepted the request,

36
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:52,660
you can enable these rules.

37
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:58,260
You can ignore all responses with a 304 status code.

38
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:03,540
304 status code means to use a local cached copy and this is generally quite a boring response.

39
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:08,100
And again, you can configure only to intercept responses that are in your target scope.

40
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:14,660
If you create an interception rule, the way it works is you define the boolean operator that will

41
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:21,060
be used to combine it with preceding rules and then you pick the attribute that you want to set a rule

42
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:21,540
for.

43
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:26,260
So for requests, you have a whole list of different attributes like domain, IP address,

44
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:29,300
the protocol, method, URL, all these sorts of things.

45
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:34,820
You can define a rule whether to match on them and you can define does match or does not match

46
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:36,420
depending on whether you want to intercept.

47
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:42,260
The match condition can be a regular expression so you've got real flexibility in what kind of items

48
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:42,900
you match on.

49
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:49,860
So to see how this works, I'm going to turn interception back on and I'm going to browse

50
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,820
a bit around the Portswiga website.

51
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:54,980
As we do this, you will see that lots of requests happen.

52
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:57,860
Some of them are requests for Portswiga.net.

53
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:04,420
Some of them are for other domains like Google Analytics that we're not as interested in.

54
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:10,660
So what I'm going to do is create an interception rule that ignores anything that isn't on the

55
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:11,700
Portswiga.net domain.

56
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:18,020
So I'll create a rule and use the AND operator and say I want to match on the domain name

57
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:21,940
and I just want to intercept items that are on the Portswiga.net domain.

58
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,060
I've defined that rule.

59
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:28,580
It gets enabled by default.

60
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:35,380
If I now go back to interception and browse around a bit more, you will see that those requests no

61
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,660
longer get intercepted.

62
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:41,860
So now that we've narrowed down interception just to the domain that we're interested in,

63
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:48,100
you'll see that quite often as you browse around, you see requests for items that you actually aren't

64
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,740
completely interested in, even if they're on the same domain.

65
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:56,980
So here we can see we get lots of requests for this bundles URL that contains a lot of

66
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:00,820
JavaScript bundles that we maybe are not interested in intercepting all the time.

67
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:06,900
So the way we can deal with that is we can add another interception rule and we can say

68
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:13,940
I'm going to match on the URL and I want URLs that do not match that URL prefix.

69
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:20,180
So if I define this rule, it enables it by default.

70
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:28,020
If I then browse around some more, we can see that requests are intercepted for the web security URL,

71
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:32,740
but not for that bundles URL, even though those requests are still happening in the background.

72
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:42,980
So I've defined individual rules for the domain name and the URL.

73
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:45,780
A different way to do that is to use target scope.

74
00:05:46,580 --> 00:05:50,020
So I'm just going to disable those two rules so they're no longer applicable.

75
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:57,300
So I'm going to go in in the sitemap and I'm going to add portswigger to scope.

76
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:03,540
And I'm going to exclude the bundles URL from scope.

77
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:08,900
So if we look at the scope definition, we can see that portswigger.net is in scope and that

78
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:11,140
bundles URL prefix is out of scope.

79
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:16,340
So what we can now do in our proxy options is we can enable this interception rule that was there,

80
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:22,660
but disabled by default to say that we'll only intercept items when the URL is in target scope.

81
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:26,740
So if we now browse around some more, we can see this is working.

82
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:31,220
We get requests for the right URL path and we don't get anything else.

83
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:38,740
For response interception rules, there are a few additional options that don't apply to requests.

84
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:47,140
So we can define rules based on whether the request was intercepted or was modified or was annotated

85
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:49,140
in our proxy testing workflow.

86
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:57,780
We can intercept based on the status code or the content type header or the mime type or the page title.

87
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:02,580
So to see how response interception rules work, I'm going to create a rule that says

88
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:08,500
I will intercept responses only if the request was modified.

89
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:13,300
And I'm going to turn on interception responses.

90
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:19,060
So if we browse around some more, we will see that requests are being intercepted in the normal way,

91
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:25,140
but no responses. And if we edit one of the one of these requests, we then see

92
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:30,580
that the response gets stalled for interception because it matched on the rule to say that the request

93
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:31,300
had been modified.

94
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:38,180
When you're working in the interception view of burp, there are some nice helper functions

95
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:39,380
that can speed up your work.

96
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:46,340
Using the context menu or using the action menu at the top, we have some options to define

97
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:47,300
interception rules.

98
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:53,780
So we can, on any particular intercepted request, we can say, don't intercept requests to this host,

99
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:56,180
IP address, file extension or directory.

100
00:07:56,740 --> 00:08:01,860
Or if we want, we can say, do intercept just the response to this one request.

101
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:05,620
And that will override any of the interception rules that you've got configured.

102
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:13,300
So that's how to use burp proxy interception rules and all of the associated options.

